---
import Base       from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav        from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import BlogPost   from "../../components/BlogPost.astro";
import Footer     from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import { site }   from "../../site";

const slug          = "wireguard-nat-traversal-behind-cgnat-2026";
const url           = `https://meshwg.com/blog/${slug}`;
const title         = "WireGuard NAT Traversal: How VPN Peers Connect Behind CGNAT and Firewalls (2026)";
const description   = "A practical guide to how WireGuard tunnels negotiate connectivity through Carrier‑Grade NAT, stateful firewalls and double‑NAT environments using keep‑alives, endpoint roaming and optional relay servers.";
const datePublished = "2026-08-25";
const dateModified  = "2026-08-25";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why does WireGuard need PersistentKeepalive behind NAT?",
    a: "Because stateful firewalls and CGNAT devices drop UDP state after a short idle period. PersistentKeepalive sends a tiny authenticated packet every N seconds to keep the translation entry alive."
  },
  {
    q: "Can WireGuard work without a control‑plane orchestrator?",
    a: "It can, but only when at least one endpoint has a static public IP or both are behind NAT types that allow hole‑punching. In double‑NAT or symmetric NAT scenarios a control‑plane or relay is required."
  }
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Nav />

  <BlogPost
    title={title}
    description={description}
    category="engineering guide"
    datePublished={datePublished}
    dateModified={dateModified}
    readMinutes={18}
    author="MeshWG editorial team"
    authorRole="Network Architecture"
  >
    <div class="bp-intro">
      <div class="tldr-box">
        <h3>TL;DR</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>PersistentKeepalive is mandatory behind any stateful NAT or CGNAT.</strong> It keeps UDP translation entries alive by sending a 32‑byte authenticated packet every 25 seconds (15 seconds on aggressive mobile networks).</li>
          <li><strong>Endpoint Roaming updates peer endpoints automatically.</strong> WireGuard kernels adjust the stored remote address as soon as a valid packet arrives, enabling seamless Wi‑Fi ↔ cellular handovers.</li>
          <li><strong>Symmetric NATs require an external relay.</strong> When both peers sit behind symmetric NAT or double‑NAT, a TURN‑style relay (DERP) or a MeshWG control‑plane must forward traffic.</li>
          <li><strong>MTU clamping prevents fragmentation.</strong> Reduce the interface MTU to 1420 bytes (1380 bytes on mobile) and enable TCP‑MSS clamping on gateways.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
      <p>
        WireGuard’s minimal kernel implementation provides blazing‑fast encryption but intentionally omits heavy NAT‑traversal protocols. In real‑world deployments, especially behind Carrier‑Grade NAT (CGNAT) or enterprise firewalls, engineers must combine WireGuard’s built‑in primitives – PersistentKeepalive, Cryptokey Endpoint Roaming, and an out‑of‑band control plane – to achieve reliable connectivity.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Problem Statement</h2>
      <p>
        Modern ISPs allocate private IPv4 space (100.64.0.0/10) to millions of customers using CGNAT. Stateful firewalls and CGNAT devices create temporary UDP translation entries that expire after a short idle interval (20 s‑300 s). When a WireGuard peer resides behind such a device, inbound packets are dropped unless a keep‑alive keeps the state alive. Additionally, symmetric NATs allocate a unique external port per destination, breaking direct hole‑punching.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Core Mechanics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>PersistentKeepalive:</strong> A configurable interval (default 0 = disabled). Setting it to 25 seconds forces the kernel to emit empty authenticated packets, keeping NAT state alive.</li>
        <li><strong>Cryptokey Endpoint Roaming:</strong> The kernel updates the stored peer endpoint whenever a valid packet is received from a new address, allowing seamless IP changes without renegotiation.</li>
        <li><strong>Relay (DERP) Fallback:</strong> When direct UDP hole‑punching fails, the control‑plane routes traffic through an encrypted relay server that both peers can reach.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4. Architecture Diagram</h2>
      <p>
        <img src="/nat_traversal_diagram.png" alt="WireGuard NAT Traversal Diagram" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; border-radius:8px;" />
      </p>

      <h2>5. Configuration Primer</h2>
      <pre><code>[Interface]
Address = 10.200.0.1/24
ListenPort = 51820
PrivateKey = &lt;private_key&gt;

[Peer]
PublicKey = &lt;peer_public_key&gt;
Endpoint = 203.0.113.45:51820
AllowedIPs = 10.200.0.2/32
PersistentKeepalive = 25
</code></pre>
      <p>
        The <code>Endpoint</code> can be a placeholder (e.g., the last known address). WireGuard will replace it automatically after the first successful packet.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Deployment Steps</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Enable <code>PersistentKeepalive = 25</code> on all peers.</li>
        <li>Set the interface MTU to 1420 bytes (1380 bytes on mobile) to avoid fragmentation.</li>
        <li>Deploy a lightweight relay (DERP) for environments where both ends are behind symmetric NAT.</li>
        <li>Integrate a control‑plane (MeshWG) that periodically publishes each node’s latest public endpoint to the others.</li>
        <li>Validate connectivity with <code>wg show</code> and monitor NAT state tables on firewalls.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>7. Troubleshooting Checklist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Verify that <code>PersistentKeepalive</code> is non‑zero on both sides.</li>
        <li>Inspect firewall logs for UDP state expirations.</li>
        <li>Confirm MTU settings on both peers and any intermediate routers.</li>
        <li>If direct traffic fails, enable a DERP relay and re‑test.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </BlogPost>

  <Footer />
</Base>
